                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "[h1]Short version: 41%[/h1]\nLike its predecessor, Rush for Gold 2 never tries to be anything more than a very basic time management game, but this time around its levels are not enjoyable enough to save its barebones gameplay.\n\n[h1]Long version:[/h1]\nRush for Gold: California is technically the sequel of a pleasant, but forgettable time management game; the kind where you gather different resource types (in this case: food, wood, gold, coal) to clear obstacles or to build production buildings.\nI say “technically”, because California is more like a carbon copy of Alaska, with the exact same mechanics—nothing has changed, no surprises await returning players.\n\nThe biggest strength of Alaska was the level design, and this is where California falters. Plainly put, this one is boring: almost every level has a similar layout, similar goals, and similar order of tasks. Admittedly, this kind of repetition has always been an integral part of the genre, but Rush for Gold 2 takes it just a few steps beyond enjoyable.",
